  1063: 
  1064:    NOTES
  1065:    1 - TS=Tri-State; OC=Open Collector; TP=Totem-Pole; PU=Pull-up; PD=Pull-down;
  1066:    VS=Vendor specific
  1067:    2 - All resistor values are minimum (lowest) allowed.
  1068:    3 - ATA-2 defines this line to be tri-stated whenever the device is not selected or is not
  1069:    executing a DMA data transfer. When enabled by DMA transfer, it shall be driven high
  1070:    and low by the device.
  1071:    4 - This signal should only be enabled during DIOR/DIOW cycles to the selected
  1072:    device.
  1073:    5 - See signal descriptions for information on dual use of this signal.
  1074:    6 - When used as CSEL, Line is grounded at Host and 10K Pull-up is required at both
  1075:    devices.
  1076:    7 - When used as SPSYNC, application is vendor specific.
  1077: 
  1078: 5. Interface signal assignments and descriptions
  1079: 
  1080: 5.1 Signal summary
  1081: 
  1082: The physical interface consists of receivers and drivers communicating through a 40-conductor flat ribbon
  1255: 5.2.5 DD0-DD15 (Device Data)
  1256: 
  1257: This is an 8- or 16-bit bi-directional data interface between the host and the device. The lower 8 bits are
  1258: used for 8-bit transfers e.g., registers, vendor specific bytes associated with the READ LONG and WRITE
  1259: LONG commands and, if the device supports the Features register capability to enable 8-bit-only data
  1260: transfers (see 8.24, Set features).
  1261: 
  1262: 5.2.6 DIOR- (Device I/O read)
  1263: 
  1264: This is the read strobe signal from the host. The falling edge of DIOR- enables data from a register or data
  1265: port of the device onto the signals, DD0-DD7 or DD0-DD15. The rising edge of DIOR- latches data at the
  1266: host and the host shall not act on the data until it is latched.
  1267: 
  1268: 5.2.7 DIOW- (Device I/O write)
  1269: 
  1270: This is the Write strobe signal from the host. The rising edge of DIOW- latches data from the signals, DD0-
  1271: DD7 or DD0-DD15, into a register or the data port of the device. The device shall not act on the data until it
  1272: is latched.
  1563: 
  1564: 6.2.4 Cylinder low register
  1565: 
  1566: In CHS Mode, this register contains the low order bits of the starting cylinder address for any media access.
  1567: In LBA Mode, this register contains Bits 15-8 of the LBA for any media access.
  1568: 
  1569: This register shall be updated to reflect the media address of the error when a media access command is
  1570: unsuccessfully completed.
  1571: 
  1572:      NOTE 9 - Prior to the development of this standard, this register was updated at the end of every
  1573:      media access command to reflect the current media address.
  1574: 
  1575: 6.2.5 Data register
  1576: 
  1577: The data register is either 8-bits or 16-bits depending on the interface width currently selected and/or the type
  1578: of data being transferred by the current command.
  1579: 
  1580: 6.2.6 Device control register
  1581: 
  1582: The bits in this register are as follows:
  1583: 
  1584:                             765432               1  0
  1585: 
  1586:                             r r r r r SRST nIEN 0
  1587: 
  1588: � Bits 7 through 3 are reserved;
  1589: � SRST is the host software reset bit. The device is held reset when this bit is set. If two devices are daisy
  1590: 
  1591:      chained on the interface, this bit resets both simultaneously;
  1592: 
  1593: � nIEN is the enable bit for the device interrupt to the host. When the nIEN bit is equal to zero, and the
  1594:      device is selected, INTRQ shall be enabled through a tri-state buffer. When the nIEN bit is equal to one,
  1595:      or the device is not selected, the INTRQ signal shall be in a high impedance state.
  1596: 
  1597: 6.2.7 Device/Head register
  1598: 
  1599: This register contains device addressing and sector addressing information.
  1600: 
  1601:                      7654                  3  2  1  0
  1602: 
  1603:                      r L r DEV HS3 HS2 HS1 HS0
  1604: 
  1605: � Bit 7 is reserved;
  1606: � L is the sector address mode select. When the L bit is equal to zero, addressing is by CHS mode.
  1607: 
  1608:      When the L bit is equal to one, addressing is by LBA mode;
  1609: 
  1610: � Bit 5 is reserved;
  1611: � DEV is the device address. When the DEV bit is equal to zero, Device 0 is selected. When the DEV bit
  1612: 
  1613:      is equal to one, Device 1 is selected;
  1614: 
  1615: � If the L bit is equal to zero (CHS Mode), the HS3 through HS0 bits contain the head address of the CHS
  1616:      address. The HS3 bit is the most significant bit. If the L bit is equal to one (LBA Mode), the HS3 through
  1617:      HS0 bits contain bits 27 through 24 of the LBA. This field shall be updated to reflect the media address of
  1618:      the error when a media access command is unsuccessfully completed.
  1619: 
  1620: NOTE 10 - Prior to the development of this standard, the head field (the HS3 through HS0 bits) of
  1621: this register was updated at the end of every media access command to reflect the current media
  1622: address.
  1623: 
  1624:                                                                                                    17
  1706: 6.2.12 Status register
  1707: 
  1708: This register contains the device status. The contents of this register are updated to reflect the current state
  1709: of the device and the progress of any command being executed by the device. When the BSY bit is equal to
  1710: zero, the other bits in this register are valid and the other Command Block register may contain meaningful
  1711: information. When the BSY bit is equal to one, no other bits in this register and all other Command Block
  1712: registers are not valid.
  1713: 
  1714:      NOTE 12 - Although host systems might be capable of generating read cycles shorter than the 400
  1715:      ns specified for status update following the last command or data cycle, host implementations should
  1716:      wait at least 400 ns before reading the status register to insure that the BSY bit is valid.
  1717: 
  1718: For devices that implement the Power Management features, the contents of the Status register and all other
  1719: Command Block registers are not valid while a device is in the Sleep mode.
  1720: 
  1721: If the host reads this register when an interrupt is pending, the interrupt is cleared.
  1722: 
  1723: 7  6  54  3  2  1  0
  1724: 
  1725: BSY DRDY DF DSC DRQ CORR IDX ERR
  1726: 
  1727: � BSY (Busy) is set whenever the device has control of the command Block Registers. When the BSY bit
  1728:      is equal to one, a write to a command block register by the host shall be ignored by the device.
